      "content": "Most high-speed compression algorithms force an uncomfortable trade-off between throughput and memory safety. To squeeze out maximum decompression speed, engines frequently omit boundary checks on malformed payloads, leaving servers vulnerable to out-of-bounds reads and memory crashes.\n\nLZAV challenges this status quo by implementing a header-only, scalar C and C++ compression engine that guarantees complete out-of-bounds safety without tanking throughput. It clocks decompression speeds exceeding 3000 megabytes per second and compression over 540 megabytes per second, outperforming LZ4 and Snappy on compression ratio while remaining fully deterministic.\n\nBecause the decompressor safely handles corrupted chunks without generating undefined behavior, systems using it can safely bypass calculating and validating uncompressed block checksums. This reduces end-to-end CPU cycles substantially in distributed storage and network serialization pipelines.\n\nSafety does not require sacrificing raw speed when your memory access bounds are designed correctly from day one."
