      "image": null,
      "content": "Pointers are not simply integer memory addresses, and treating them as such will eventually trigger catastrophic compiler miscompilations. Modern optimizing compilers in both Rust and C++ rely heavily on pointer provenance to deduce whether two memory locations can legally alias.\n\nWhen a compiler sees an opaque function call receiving an arbitrary pointer alongside an isolated local variable, it assumes the pointer cannot modify the local value unless it was explicitly derived from it. Even if two pointers happen to hold the identical bit pattern representing a hardware memory address, dereferencing one does not mean the compiler permits aliasing the allocation of the other.\n\nWithout provenance rules, compilers would be forced to invalidate cached registers across every function boundary, crippling runtime performance. Understanding provenance clarifies why casting pointers to integer types and back causes subtle undefined behavior during optimization passes.\n\nWriting robust low-level systems software requires thinking in terms of abstract allocation boundaries rather than flat linear hardware bytes."
