      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50382763",
      "image": null,
      "content": "PostgreSQL stores rows within fixed 8-kilobyte data pages, which means that any column value exceeding that boundary cannot fit in place. When a tuple exceeds roughly two kilobytes, the engine offloads large values to a dedicated shadow table using The Oversized-Attribute Storage Technique, commonly known as TOAST.\n\nThis mechanism splits oversized values like large JSON documents or raw text into small chunks stored in a background relation. The primary table only retains a pointer to those external chunks. While this keeps primary row sizes compact, it introduces significant hidden costs.\n\nEvery sequential scan or row update touching that column must now perform secondary lookups against the TOAST table. If the data is compressed, CPU cycles are spent repeatedly decompressing the payload upon retrieval. Even worse, updating an unrelated column in that row can still trigger overhead if the system does not handle the pointer references efficiently.\n\nArchitecting relational schemas around massive blobs often destroys query latency and bloats your buffer cache. Storing large unstructured payloads outside the transactional path or strictly in external object stores is often the best remedy.\n\nNever treat unconstrained text columns as free storage in transactional databases."
    },
    {
