      "image": null,
      "content": "Every database engineer has experienced the dread of a query that ran in milliseconds for months suddenly timing out overnight after a routine table analyze. When data volume shifts slightly, the query planner recalculates costs and can flip an optimal nested loop or hash join into an inefficient sequential scan.\n\nPostgreSQL 19 addresses this classic stability problem with two modules named pg_plan_advice and pg_stash_advice. The new tooling allows the planner to export its chosen execution plan as a structured string via explain commands, and store that plan keyed by query identifier. When enabled, the engine enforces the saved plan on future query executions instead of recomputing a new path from scratch.\n\nThis mechanism brings the equivalent of query plan baselines directly to PostgreSQL without requiring invasive query text modifications. You can now lock known-good execution paths in production, insulating critical endpoints from unpredictable optimizer decisions.\n\nPlan stability turns database performance from an unpredictable moving target into a deterministic operational guarantee."
    },
