      "content": "Most analytical query engines treat object storage queries as simple HTTP GET requests with byte ranges. That naive approach quickly turns expensive and slow when queries run concurrently over flaky or latency-sensitive network connections.\n\nHaybarn 2.0 brings concrete network-level improvements to DuckDB 2.0. Instead of opening a brand new HTTP/1.1 connection for every single chunk, its custom httpfs layer leverages HTTP/2 multiplexing so a multithreaded scan streams multiple range reads across one connection. Furthermore, range reads send the file ETag as an If-Match precondition. If an underlying file changes mid-query, the remote server immediately rejects the request with a 412 status code before transmitting gigabytes of wasted data.\n\nThese engineering adjustments cut network waste, prevent data corruption during continuous ETL updates, and significantly speed up startup times in WebAssembly environments.\n\nTreating network protocols as first-class citizens inside query execution yields massive performance wins."
