      "content": "Almost every enterprise data warehouse and catalog defaults to marking columns with a simple boolean flag: pii: true or pii: false. In real-world data platforms subject to GDPR or compliance audits, collapsing privacy classification into a single bit discards critical architectural signal.\n\nA single boolean cannot answer whether an attribute is an isolated unique identifier, a quasi-identifier that only exposes identity when joined, or whether the underlying field is raw, pseudonymized, or fully anonymized. If your audit logs cannot prove whether a reverse mapping exists, your compliance guarantees fall apart.\n\nTo build a defensible data catalog, you must treat sensitivity and transformation state as orthogonal dimensions. Classify columns by identifier strength (direct, quasi, non-identifying) alongside their technical state (plaintext, salted hash, differential privacy output), backed by strict provenance tracking.\n\nDesigning metadata schemas with granular privacy vectors eliminates downstream audit ambiguities and protects your pipelines from costly retroactive migrations."
