      "content": "Autonomous coding agents like Claude Code and Cursor run shell commands and access local filesystems by default. Without isolation, an agent tricked by prompt injection can inspect ~/.aws/credentials or read your private keys and transmit them externally.\n\nAgentShield approaches this problem from the operating system layer rather than application middleware. On Linux, it combines unprivileged Bubblewrap containers with Landlock Linux Security Modules to enforce read-only root filesystems, PID namespace isolation, and network egress limits down to explicit CIDRs.\n\nOn macOS, it leverages Darwin Seatbelt profiles to halt unauthorized Mach messaging and sysctl queries, while Windows runs processes under low-integrity AppContainers. Decoy canaries sit in memory to spot credential harvesting before a network call can fire.\n\nApplying deterministic kernel primitives rather than probabilistic model prompt guards provides genuine containment for local developer tooling."
