      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50378080",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Algorithmic pricing research has long assumed that repeated Bertrand competition inevitably drives prices toward marginal cost. New research examining LLM pricing agents in homogeneous markets demonstrates that language model agents routinely break this assumption, escaping marginal cost pricing entirely.\n\nThe divergence does not come from hidden collusion logic inside the weights. It depends on deployment interfaces. Agents that consume raw market histories with rival prices and emit discrete price points regularly establish dynamic high-price anchors, undercutting competitors just enough to take volume while holding prices high.\n\nConversely, when agents emit probability distributions over summarized profit histories, self-play drives them straight back to traditional competitive Nash equilibria. The information harness and action space dictate the economic outcome.\n\nFor engineers deploying autonomous agents in dynamic environments, system boundaries and input formatting define macro behavior just as much as model intelligence."
