      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://alnovis.io/blog/aggregate-is-a-function",
      "date": "2026-11-05",
      "authors": [
        "Alexander Novikov"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn why naive running sums fail when processing real-world event streams with duplicate and delayed data. This article explores how to architect robust, low-latency analytical aggregations in ClickHouse.",
      "hn_id": "50382974",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50382974",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Treating an analytical aggregate as a running sum is one of the quickest ways to corrupt a reporting pipeline. In an ideal world, streaming data arrives sequentially and exactly once, making ClickHouse ingestion straightforward. In production, networks drop packets, Kafka delivers at-least-once, and edge registers send backlogged data weeks late.\n\nWhen consumers retry failed batches, duplicate receipts enter the table. A naive running sum counts those duplicate records forever, while recomputing everything from wide raw tables forces the database to scan forty-six million rows just to aggregate five hundred thousand.\n\nThe fundamental shift is treating an aggregate not as an accumulating counter, but as a pure mathematical function over an identifiable set of business keys. If the pipeline cannot assume ordered or unique delivery, the storage layer must embrace idempotent state representations.\n\nRelying on specialized ClickHouse aggregate state engines allows intermediate rollups to merge deterministically. Late-arriving partitions update without invalidating previous summaries, and duplicate stream offsets resolve without double counting.\n\nIdempotency must live in the aggregation function itself, not in the delivery transport."
    },
