      "content": "Building APIs for autonomous agents requires discarding human-centric assumptions. Standard single-page web consoles break immediately because the typical fetch tool inside an agent harness does not execute client-side JavaScript, leaving the model with an empty shell.\n\nDesigning services for agent use without complex protocols like MCP requires shifting to strict server-rendered HTML or plain JSON endpoints. The fetch tool is the true user. Most agent execution failures occur before the model even begins reasoning because authentication hurdles, dynamic redirection, or missing context obscure the target action.\n\nExposing self-descriptive URLs, embedding next-step instructions directly into responses, and removing interactive logins lets coding agents inspect and trigger webhooks effortlessly. Treating standard HTTP as the primary agent interface avoids protocol bloat while boosting task completion rates.\n\nDesigning for machines forces you to deliver clean, predictable APIs that benefit human developers just as much as autonomous tools."
